---
import DocLayout from '../../components/DocLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/url';
---

<DocLayout
  title="Troubleshooting"
  description="Fix first-launch blocks, router key errors, port conflicts, missing language servers, and database recovery in Sarthi Studio."
>
  <h2>The OS blocks the first launch</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>macOS Gatekeeper:</strong> right-click the app → Open → Open.
      Unsigned builds are blocked on double-click only the first time.
      See <a href={withBase('/download/macos/')}>macOS downloads</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Windows SmartScreen:</strong> More info → Run anyway, after
      verifying the SHA-256. See <a href={withBase('/download/windows/')}>Windows downloads</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Android:</strong> allow “install unknown apps” for the app
      you opened the APK with. See <a href={withBase('/download/android/')}>Android downloads</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>“Add your router's unified API key in Settings first”</h2>
  <p>
    The connected router has no key configured in Studio. Open Settings →
    Router, paste the router's unified API key, and run the connection
    test. With FreeLLMAPI, create or copy the key in the router console
    first. Provider keys belong in the router, not in Studio.
  </p>

  <h2>The window is blank or the app will not start (Linux)</h2>
  <p>
    The native window needs WebKitGTK 4.1. Install your distribution's
    package (<code>libwebkit2gtk-4.1-0</code> on Debian/Ubuntu,
    <code>webkit2gtk4.1</code> on Arch), or run
    <code>SAARTHI-STUDIO --browser</code> to use the identical UI in your
    browser while you sort the dependency out.
  </p>

  <h2>Port 8765 is already in use</h2>
  <p>
    Another Studio instance (or another service) holds the port. Close
    the other instance, or choose a different port:
    <code>SAARTHI_STUDIO_PORT=9000 SAARTHI-STUDIO</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>No diagnostics in the Problems panel</h2>
  <p>
    A language server must be installed and configured for the language.
    Add one in Settings (for example <code>rust-analyzer</code> for Rust);
    if the binary is not on PATH, point the server entry at its saved
    location. The first analysis of a large project can take a few
    seconds — an empty first publish is normal before real diagnostics
    arrive.
  </p>

  <h2>The database will not open</h2>
  <p>
    Studio refuses to run on a corrupt database rather than risk your
    history, and offers recovery. Keep the corrupt file, let the recovery
    path rebuild what it can, and report what happened with the file
    attached if you can share it. Never delete the database to “fix” a
    startup error unless you intend to lose the history.
  </p>

  <h2>Still stuck?</h2>
  <p>
    Open an issue with your platform, the release version, and what you
    see. The repository's issue tracker is public, and the feature-status
    report lists known limitations so you can check whether what you hit
    is a bug or a documented gap.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
